  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis this afternoon highlight \nan upper wave continuing its lift into southern Canada with \nmostly- zonal flow aloft situated over the Northern and Central \nPlains. At the surface, a cold front spans from the Oklahoma \nPanhandle through central Kansas and into the Great Lakes. \nShowers have developed near this boundary this afternoon and \ncontinue to track to the east/southeast across the forecast \narea. Could also see a few weak thunderstorms try to develop \nacross central Kansas later this afternoon into the evening \ngiven modest elevated instability. Despite inverted-v point \nsoundings per short-term guidance, meager elevated lapse rates \nshould preclude severe activity, although brief gusty winds in \nthe strongest activity can't be ruled out heading into the \novernight hours. \n\nIsolated showers and storms will look to continue overnight \nacross central Kansas in response to increasing 850/700 mb warm-\nair advection. Heading toward morning, these will shift into \nsoutheast Kansas along with the surface boundary, though \ncoverage should remain greatest across northern Kansas into \nsouthern Nebraska during this timeframe where greater column \nmoisture will reside. Additionally, chances for showers and \nstorms will return by Wednesday evening/night as another upper \nwave tracks across the Northern Plains. Short-term guidance \nhighlights increasing instability mainly across eastern Kansas \ninto western Missouri, and combined with increasing mid-level \nwinds resulting in solid shear for this time of year, strong to \nmarginally severe storms may develop across eastern portions of \nour forecast area. Afternoon highs will likely vary from north \nto south, with values ranging from near 80 across central Kansas\nto the middle 90s across southern Kansas. \n\nIsolated to scattered activity will continue into Thursday morning \nwith warm-air advection persisting over the region. Decreasing cloud \ncover throughout the day may allow for high temperatures back in the \nlow to middle 90s, though as the prior cycle pointed out, guidance \nmay be just a bit on the hot side considering potential cloud \ncoverage and the stalled out front lingering near the \nKansas/Oklahoma border. Another round of showers and storms is \nexpected Thursday evening/night amid more mid-level warm-air \nadvection, and with returning solid mid-level flow and instability, \na handful of strong to marginally severe storms can't be ruled out.\n\nActivity should exit the area by Friday afternoon and give way to \nmostly dry conditions through the weekend. From here, long-range \nguidance continues to highlight a return of dry weather as upper \nlevel ridging commences over the region. This may promote highs in \nthe 90s and perhaps even low 100s across portions of the forecast \narea this coming weekend and beyond.",
